.TH "@" 1 "" "New Human Shell"
.SH NAME
@ \- run a New Human Shell request
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B @
runs one request using the New Human Shell prose program.
Requests can combine English, Unix commands, and symbols. Standard input
supplies data when the request consumes it. A filter writes its result to
standard output.
With no request or input, the prose program directs the runner to print
.BR "α | Ω" .
.PP
The loader for
.B @
is
.BR LD (1).
To invoke it explicitly, use
.BR "LD @" .
Arguments such as
.B \-c
are request text. An optional leading
.B \-\-
separates the request from the loader. Quote a multiline request as a
single argument to preserve its line breaks.
.PP
For a standalone request, the loader uses
.BR lordhavemercy (1)
by default. Set
.B LD_RUNNER
to select another runner.
.SH REQUEST SYNTAX
A bare
.B @
between stages passes the result of each stage to the next, from left to
right. Quote the whole request when entering this form at a Bash prompt.
A bare
.B @
in an ordinary Bash command can be a literal argument.
Quote a literal
.B @
inside the request when it could be read as an operator.
When the request describes a stream transform, the result goes to standard
output. Editing the source file requires an explicit request.
.PP
.BI @ name
can select a named scope.
.B @@
names the shell's own vocabulary. The shell uses the current context and
saved definitions to resolve these names and may ask for clarification.
.B ibid
carries over the last applicable referent or pattern in the current scope.
Confirmed reusable meanings are saved in the user's prefix for later
sessions.
.SH SHELL HOST
Start
.B nsh
at a terminal or supply lines on standard input. Use
.B nhs
for the same host with
.B >>>
and
.B ...
prompts. Use
.BI "nsh \-c " line
to submit one shell line and return its resulting status. The arguments
after the line set
.B $0
and then the positional parameters, if supplied.
.PP
The host runs ordinary input through Bash first. It displays captured
standard error after Bash returns and delegates a nonzero line status to
.BR law (1),
even when Bash emits no diagnostic. Use
.B @
as a command to request prose interpretation explicitly.
A bare command name such as
.B review
that resolves on
.B PATH
to a readable, executable file beginning with
.B "#!/usr/bin/env LD"
is tried through
.BR LD (1)
with the command name as its request. If that attempt fails, the host
evaluates the original line through Bash.
.PP
The host supplies the current directory, the original failed Bash line
with its starting directory, status, and available standard error,
an excerpt of the saved definitions, up to 16 recent turns, up to 16
commands from saved terminal history, and any pending question.
.SH HOSTED REPLIES
The host interprets these replies from a runner that exits successfully.
Put the marker on the first line by itself.
.TP
.B @ask
Put the question on the following line or lines. The host displays it on
standard error and sets status 2. It retains the original failure and sends
the next input to the runner as the answer, without evaluating it in Bash.
.TP
.B @fail
Put the diagnostic on the following line or lines. The host displays it on
standard error and returns the original Bash status.
.TP
.B @more
A reply consisting of this marker tries the next runner in the order
listed in
.BR lord (1).
If all six ask for more effort, the host reports the failure and returns
the original Bash status.
.PP
An ordinary reply goes to standard output. The host checks for updated
Bash declarations in
.I etc/nsh.bash
and clears Bash's command lookup cache.
A runner error stops the sequence and returns the runner's status.
.SH INPUT AND PROMPTS
Type
.B :paste
to enter a block ending at a blank line. During a pending question, ending
the first line of an answer with a colon also starts a block. Use
.BI "ld.so " command
to run a native command and return its status even while a question is
pending. At a terminal, Readline provides line editing and history recall.
Ctrl+C cancels the current input, command, block, or question. Ctrl+D at an
empty prompt exits.
.PP
With colors enabled, the working directory is shown in cyan in the
.B nsh
prompt. The prompt marker is
green after success, yellow during a pending question, red after failure,
and magenta when collecting other multiline input.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NSH_HOME
Directory for learned vocabulary and shell state. The default is
.IR ~/.nsh .
.TP
.B LD_RUNNER
Runner selected by
.BR LD (1)
for direct prose-program invocations. Delegated Bash failures begin with
.BR law (1).
.TP
.B NSH_PROGRAM
Prose program path used by the shell host. If unset or empty, the host
looks for
.B @
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to a nonempty value to disable shell colors.
.SH FILES
Paths below are relative to
.BR NSH_HOME .
.TP
.I etc/nshrc
Saved definitions written as UTF-8 prose, one scoped meaning per line.
Use the form
.RI "scope | term means " description .
.TP
.I etc/nsh.bash
Optional Bash declarations. The shell host loads them at startup and
checks for changes after an ordinary successful agent reply.
.TP
.I bin/
Learned executables. The shell host adds this directory to its search path.
.TP
.I lib/
Code or data shared by learned commands.
.TP
.I history
Terminal command history. A line beginning with a space stays out of
history and the host's recent turn list.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
echo @ | @ ot
@ 'echo 木 @ pinyin @ uppercase'
@ "cat 'this prompt' @ sed 's/write a prompt/do this prompt/'"
cat 'this prompt' | @ sed 's/write a prompt/do this prompt/'
.EE
.PP
The first example prints
.BR yhwh .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR LD (1),
.BR lord (1),
.BR bash (1)
